  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The predictive validity of the cognitive screen was further constrained by the participants' years of education and communication limitations.",
      "what": "This prospective cohort study evaluated the predictive validity of the Addenbrooke's Cognitive Examination III (ACE-III) against a standardized on-road driving assessment for 133 drivers aged 33 to 97 referred to a specialist clinic between August 2022 and June 2024. Using a two-cut-off model where scores of 81 or lower predicted failure and 88 or higher predicted passing, the cognitive screen achieved an overall accuracy of 79% and an area under the curve of 0.81. However, misclassification was notable: 27% of drivers classified as unimpaired failed the road test, 18% classified as impaired passed, and 26% fell into an indeterminate grey zone.",
      "why": "The study demonstrates that the ACE-III is not accurate enough to serve as a standalone tool for determining fitness-to-drive, establishing that on-road evaluations must remain the reference standard for high-stakes licensing decisions."
    }
  },
